# Shell scripts are read by bash, which counts a carriage return as part of
# the word: `set -u\r` is an invalid option, not a spelling of `set -u`. On a
# Windows checkout core.autocrlf would otherwise hand bash CRLF files and the
# checks that run them - completions_check, the fuzz builds, package.sh - fail
# for the reason alone. eol=lf pins them on the way out and keeps a Windows
# commit from storing CRLF in them.
*.sh    text eol=lf
*.bash  text eol=lf
*.zsh   text eol=lf

# The scenario tree is compared byte for byte: a fixture, a golden or a mock
# asset that picks up a carriage return on checkout makes the case fail with a
# diff in which every line looks identical. On a Windows checkout eight of them
# did exactly that - compat/install, compat/update, compat/config-gate,
# install/checksum-ok, install/checksum-mismatch, sig/basic-ape,
# sig/verified-required, txn/crash-before-manifest - and pass with the same
# binary once the tree is LF. Nothing here holds a carriage return in the
# index, so this changes no stored byte: it only says what a checkout must
# hand over.
src/test/scenarios/** text eol=lf

# Carriage returns here are the input under test: the HTTP parser is being
# asked to survive CRLF line endings and malformed ones. Normalising them to
# LF would quietly delete the case every one of these seeds makes.
src/test/fuzz/corpus/http/** -text
src/test/fuzz/corpus/checksums/** -text
src/test/fuzz/corpus/json/** -text
